At hadron colliders, the production of vector bosons ($W$ and $Z$) in forward-direction muon collisions serves as a rare process ideal for study within high-luminosity muon collision experiments. Due to the inherently low cross-sections of this reaction, statistical biases often dominate asymmetry measurements. To address this, we introduce a new method for assessing forward muon polarization asymmetries, targeting the $P_{\mu-}$ and $A_C^{(\pm)}$ quantities. This approach utilizes a sample of charged current (CC) and neutral current (NC) asymmetries. We have determined this new method's performance using pseudo-data generated from $\pi^+$ and $K^+$ fixed-target experiments, as well as proton-proton and neutron-proton collisions at $\sqrt{s}=200$~GeV. The method demonstrates superior capability in determining $A_C^{(-)}$ from $B+`$ and $B-`$ asymmetries and in evaluating neutron polarization relative to both $K^+$ and $\pi^+$ beams. Given that this technique relies on absolute asymmetry measurements, it is particularly well-suited for future high-precision polarization asymmetry experiments, such as those planned for a $K^+-\mu^+$ fixed-target setup at the proposed International Muon Laboratory. Specifically, this new method could help determine $A_C^{(+)}$ from muon and antimuon charging current asymmetries in future $K^+-\mu^-$ beams. Furthermore, it will enable the measurement of neutron polarization in $K^-$, $\pi^-$, and future antiproton beams. We have also adapted this method for use with neutrino and antineutrino data. For this adaptation, we propose determining the absolute polarization of future neutrino and antineutrino beams through muon and charged pion asymmetries in $\mu^+-\pi^-$ and $\mu^--\pi^+$ collisions. This can be achieved using a mixed beam composition with a known polarization, for instance, a $
